# SitePro Backend

When working inside `backend/api`:

- Read and follow `backend/api/AGENTS.md`.
- Treat Laravel as the authoritative SitePro backend and domain layer.
- Inspect the existing implementation and relevant migrations before making
  changes.
- Existing committed migrations are the current database architecture
  baseline.
- Do not modify existing migrations or redesign established tables without
  an explicit database architecture requirement.
- Enforce authorization, validation and tenant isolation on the backend.
- Never rely on frontend restrictions for security.
- Preserve account, membership, website and tenant boundaries.
- Treat authentication, permissions, domains, draft content, publishing,
  billing and AI mutations as security-sensitive.
- AI operations must follow normal SitePro authorization, validation,
  persistence and publishing rules.
- Prefer established Laravel conventions over custom architectural layers.
- Do not introduce repositories, services, actions or other abstractions
  automatically; use them when they provide a clear responsibility.
- Keep API contracts explicit and predictable for the independently deployed
  SitePro frontend applications.
- Do not invent product behavior or API contracts.
- Consult relevant `/docs` files when product or architecture behavior is
  involved.
- Add or update appropriate tests when behavior changes.
